<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Włocławek</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Włocławek is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, in the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>. From the 12th century it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Kuyavia, whose Gothic cathedral still dominates the town. It grew as a river port and grain-trading centre and later became an industrial town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wloclawek.jpg" alt="Włocławek" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aneta Pawska · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bazylika_katedralna_WNMP_we_W%C5%82oc%C5%82awku,_widok_z_drona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Włocławek lies along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in the Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship, bordered by the Gostynin-Włocławek Landscape Park. It is the third largest city of the voivodeship, after Bydgoszcz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, and the main cultural and economic centre of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>. Excavations on the site have uncovered a settlement of the Lusatian culture from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, evidence of early Pomeranian culture, and traces of settlement from the Roman period and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement was founded in the 9th century, and in the 10th century it became part of the emerging Polish state under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a>. The Gesta principum Polonorum, a chronicle of the 1110s, gives the oldest known mention of the city and calls it one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s most important administrative centres under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a>. The diocese of Włocławek is recorded in a bull of Pope Eugene III in 1148, with Warner as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. The city received its rights in 1255. In the 14th and 15th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> destroyed and captured it several times.</p><p>Its later prosperity came from the river transport of grain, tolls on grain floated down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, the shipment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> for export to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and brewing. Bishop Stanisław Karnkowski founded a theological seminary in 1569. The Swedes ravaged the city in 1657 and the Russians in 1707. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a> in 1793 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, then to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1807 and to the Congress Kingdom of Poland in 1815. The first faience factory opened in 1873. In 1920 Polish forces defended the city against the Soviets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Włocławek is the seat of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Włocławek, one of the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and its Gothic cathedral is listed as a Historic Monument of Poland. The city is known for its production of faience and ketchup. Poland's largest hydroelectric power plant is located there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Włocławek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kuyavia was rich in farmland and salt, and Kruszwica and <b>Wloclawek</b>, seat of the bishops of Kuyavia, were important centres.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-kuyavia">Duchy of Kuyavia</a></li></ul></section>
